  "query_preview": "..\nA monsoon surge continues well to the south of Typhoon Dolphin, which\nis currently centered east of Iwo To near 25N147E. Sustained west\nwinds and gusts picked up today as heavy showers and thunderstorms\ndeveloped across the Marianas, with sustained winds briefly reaching\n28 mph at the Guam airport, and up to 20 mph on Saipan. These\nconditions will continue into midweek, with a peak in winds \nanticipated Tuesday across the Marianas. Model guidance and \nobservational trends indicate the highest winds will be felt over \nGuam and its coastal waters, and a Wind Advisory remains in effect\nfor Guam through early Wednesday morning, with sustained winds of 20\nto 30 mph expected with gusts to around 40 mph, mainly near the\nheaviest showers. Winds are expected to relax Wednesday night through\nFriday as a col area develops just north of the region, but this is\ndependent on the development and movement of a secondary, weak \ndisturbance along the eastern extent of the monsoon trough, north of \nthe Marshall Islands. Models depict an elongated circulation\nbeginning to develop somewhere near Wake Island on Tuesday, but as a\ndisturbance has not yet formed, uncertainty remains somewhat high in\nthe wind forecast toward the end of the week. Visible satellite \nimagery and CIMSS 850 mb vorticity analysis do show an area of low- \nlevel vorticity, co-located with developing convection to the north \nof Majuro. As a disturbance develops, uncertainty will decrease with \nregards to the regional pressure gradient and broader wind field.\nLate this weekend into early next week, the monsoon pattern looks to\nresurge across the Marianas, which will reinforce marine and surf\nhazards, discussed below.",
